				\begin{document}$${\hat{T}={argmi}{n}_{T}E}_{X,Y}L\left(Y,T\left(X\;\right)\right)$$\end{document}T^=argminTEX,YLY,TXwhere X and Y are the feature and response data, respectively, and \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$L$$\end{document}L is the loss function of choice. In our setting, the loss function was chosen to be a negative likelihood compatible with the typical distributional assumptions for each type of data. Specifically, the best overall response was modeled using the logistic regression likelihood, while progression-free survival and overall survival were modeled using the Cox proportional hazards likelihood.
